<section id="what-is" class="py-20 md:py-24 bg-soil-100 dark:bg-rock-950/50">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl text-center
      text-soil-900 dark:text-cream">
      What is <span class="font-mono">vgi-rpc</span>?
    </h2>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-soil-900 dark:text-cream">
      <p>
        <strong class="font-mono">vgi-rpc</strong> is an RPC framework where you define services as{' '}
        <strong>typed interfaces</strong> &mdash; the framework automatically derives{' '}
        <a href="https://arrow.apache.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="qf-link">
          Apache Arrow
        </a> schemas from your type annotations and handles all serialization.
      </p>

      <p>
        You write a Protocol class in Python, or the equivalent interface in Go, TypeScript, Rust, Java, C#, or C++.
        There are <strong>no <code class="rounded border border-soil-300/70 bg-white/70 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] dark:border-rock-700 dark:bg-rock-800">.proto</code> files</strong>{' '}
        and no separate IDL; the class is the contract. The <strong>same interface works across every transport</strong>:
        in-process pipes, subprocess stdin/stdout, Unix and TCP sockets, shared memory, HTTP, and authenticated Iroh/QUIC.
        Moving a service from a subprocess to HTTP changes only the address the client passes to{' '}
        <code class="rounded border border-soil-300/70 bg-white/70 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] dark:border-rock-700 dark:bg-rock-800">connect()</code>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Calls are <strong>unary</strong>, <strong>producer streams</strong> (the server pushes batches),
        or <strong>exchange streams</strong> (lockstep bidirectional). Errors, logs, and OpenTelemetry traces travel with them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
